  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 252 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nEarly This Morning...\n\nThe meandering front is currently across the southwest forecast \narea. Some warm advection aloft may interact with the front to \nproduce a few sprinkles/isolated showers, but for now confidence is \nnot high enough to include.\n\nToday...\n\nLow pressure in Iowa will bring the warm front back northeast across \nat least part of the area today. Meanwhile, an upper wave will move \nthrough today. Forcing ahead of the upper wave has already generated \na thunderstorm complex across southern Wisconsin.\n\nForcing from the aforementioned features should be enough to \ninteract with the abundant moisture in the area to produce scattered \nto numerous showers and thunderstorms today. \n\nThe thunderstorms well to the northwest early this morning will \nslide southeast in the upper flow. With the forcing, would expect it \nto survive into Indiana. After that though, there are plenty of \nquestions on coverage and location of the storms. \n\nThe best forcing will remain north of central Indiana, so would \nexpect the best chances for the storms to continue will be across \nnorthern Indiana into the northeast forecast area. If the storms \narrive before much instability can build this morning, they might \nweaken and become more scattered. Later arrival and thus better \ninstability would hold them together longer. \n\nWhile the latest HRRR runs keep most storms northeast of central \nIndiana, most other CAMs have more coverage into the area today. \nWill go likely PoPs across about the northeast half of the area, \nwith lower PoPs southeast. Will have to monitor closely.\n\nIf the storms arrive with better instability, given the expected \ninstability and high moisture content, an isolated severe storm with \ndamaging winds is possible. \n\nTemperatures will depend on timing of storms and how far northeast \nthe boundary gets. Either way, there will be another gradient from \nnortheast to southwest with highs in the lower 80s to the lower and \nmiddle 90s.\n\nTonight and Sunday...\n\nForcing from upper energy will focus north of central Indiana again \ntonight. Will have some chance PoPs north have a dry forecast south.\n\nOn Sunday, another stronger upper wave will move through and could \nproduce scattered to numerous thunderstorms. Will have likely PoPs \nnorth and chance PoPs south. Instability will be high enough again \nfor an isolated severe storm with damaging winds.\n\nA temperature gradient will continue on Sunday, with highs in the \nupper 70s north to lower 90s far south. \n\nMonday and beyond...\n\nSunday's upper wave will push the front south of the area, but \nanother stronger wave will generate showers and perhaps a \nthunderstorm on Monday. \n\nA larger upper trough/low will move through Tuesday. Most moisture \nwill be south of the area so will only have low PoPs. Uncertainty on \nhow fast this exits will keep low PoPs in the east on Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure will then bring dry weather to end the week.\n\nWith the hot air finally pushed south, highs will be near to below \nnormal early in this period, to near normal to end the period.",
